@module flutter-m01 | Flutter Introduction
@summary Understand what Flutter is, how it works, set up your tools and run your first app.

@lesson flutter-m01-l01 | What is Flutter? | 10
## Learning objectives
- Describe Flutter and the platforms it targets
- Explain the "everything is a widget" idea
- Recognise why teams choose Flutter

## One codebase, many platforms
Flutter is Google's open-source **UI toolkit** for building natively compiled applications for **Android, iOS, web, Windows, macOS and Linux** from a single Dart codebase. It was first released as 1.0 in December 2018 and is used by companies such as Google (Google Pay, Google Earth), BMW, Toyota, Alibaba and ByteDance.

Unlike toolkits that wrap the platform's native buttons and text fields, Flutter **draws every pixel itself** using its own rendering engine. That's why a Flutter app looks the same on every device and can run smooth, custom animations at the display's full refresh rate.

## Everything is a widget
In Flutter you describe your UI by composing **widgets**: small, immutable descriptions of part of the interface. Text, padding, rows, buttons, gestures, animations and even the whole app are widgets.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

void main() {
  runApp(
    const MaterialApp(
      home: Scaffold(
        body: Center(
          child: Text('Hello, Flutter!'),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  );
}
```

Read it from the outside in: an app (`MaterialApp`) whose home screen (`Scaffold`) has a body that centres (`Center`) a piece of text (`Text`). You build UIs by **nesting** widgets like this, forming a **widget tree**.

## Declarative UI
Flutter is **declarative**: you describe *what* the UI should look like for the current state, and Flutter figures out how to update the screen. When state changes, you rebuild the description; Flutter efficiently updates only what changed. You never manually find a label and change its text.

## Why teams choose Flutter
- **Productivity**: one codebase for many platforms, plus **hot reload** to see changes instantly.
- **Performance**: Dart is compiled ahead of time to native machine code; the engine renders with the GPU.
- **Expressive UI**: full control over every pixel, rich Material 3 and Cupertino widget sets.
- **Ecosystem**: thousands of packages on pub.dev and first-party plugins for device features.

> NOTE: Bimo itself is a native Android app built with Kotlin and Jetpack Compose, a declarative toolkit with very similar ideas. Skills you learn here transfer between declarative frameworks.

:::check Does Flutter use the platform's native button widgets?
No. Flutter renders its own widgets with its engine, which is why apps look consistent across platforms.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Thinking Flutter is a web view wrapper. It compiles to native code and renders with its own engine.
- Expecting to change UI imperatively ("find this label and set its text") instead of rebuilding from state.

## Summary
- Flutter builds apps for mobile, web and desktop from one Dart codebase.
- UIs are trees of immutable widgets; Flutter draws them itself.
- It's declarative: UI = f(state).

## Further learning
- flutter.dev ▸ Docs ▸ Get started
- docs.flutter.dev ▸ Flutter architectural overview
@quiz
? mcq: Which language are Flutter apps written in?
- Kotlin
+ Dart
- JavaScript
- Swift
> Flutter apps are written in Dart.
? tf: Flutter renders its UI using the platform's native button and text components.
= false
> Flutter draws its own widgets with its rendering engine.
? fill: In Flutter, the UI is built by composing small, immutable ___.
= widgets | widget
> Everything you see is described by widgets.
? mcq: What does "declarative UI" mean?
+ You describe what the UI should look like for the current state
- You manually update individual views when data changes
- The UI is written in XML
- The UI cannot change
> In declarative frameworks the UI is a function of state.
? scenario: A startup must ship an Android and iOS app quickly with a small team and a custom-branded design. Why might Flutter fit?
+ One codebase targets both platforms with full control over custom UI
- Flutter only supports Android
- Flutter requires two separate codebases
- Flutter cannot draw custom designs
> Flutter shares code across platforms and renders custom designs consistently.

@lesson flutter-m01-l02 | Flutter architecture | 14
## Learning objectives
- Name the layers of Flutter: framework, engine, embedder
- Understand the three trees: widgets, elements, render objects
- Know what Impeller is

## The layered architecture
```diagram
┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Your app (Dart)                          │
├──────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Framework (Dart)                         │
│  Material  |  Cupertino                  │
│  Widgets                                 │
│  Rendering                               │
│  Animation | Painting | Gestures         │
│  Foundation                              │
├──────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Engine (C++): Impeller/Skia, Dart runtime,│
│ text layout, platform channels           │
├──────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Embedder (platform-specific):            │
│ Android (Java/C++), iOS (Obj-C), Windows,│
│ macOS, Linux, Web                        │
└──────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

- **Framework**: everything you import from `package:flutter`, written in Dart. You can read its source in your IDE.
- **Engine**: written mostly in C++; renders frames, runs Dart, lays out text and talks to the platform.
- **Embedder**: the platform-specific host that creates a window/surface, forwards input events and manages the app lifecycle.

## Rendering: Impeller
Flutter's modern renderer is **Impeller**, which precompiles shaders so animations don't stutter the first time they run ("shader compilation jank"). Impeller is the default on iOS and on modern Android devices; older Android devices fall back to Skia.

## Three trees
When you write widgets, Flutter maintains three parallel trees:

1. **Widget tree**: your immutable configuration (`Text('Hi')`). Cheap to create and thrown away on every rebuild.
2. **Element tree**: long-lived objects that link widgets to their place in the tree and hold `State`. Elements decide whether to update or recreate parts of the UI.
3. **RenderObject tree**: objects that perform **layout** (sizes and positions), **painting** and **hit testing**.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class Greeting extends StatelessWidget {
  const Greeting({super.key, required this.name});
  final String name;

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    // A new Text widget is created every build – that's fine, widgets are cheap.
    return Text('Hello $name');
  }
}
```

When `name` changes, `build` returns a new `Text` widget. The element compares it with the old one: same type and key → it **updates** the existing render object's text instead of creating a new one. This is why rebuilding is fast.

## The frame pipeline
Each frame (60 or 120 times per second), Flutter runs:
1. **Build**: call `build` on dirty widgets.
2. **Layout**: render objects compute sizes ("constraints go down, sizes go up").
3. **Paint**: render objects record drawing commands.
4. **Composite**: layers are sent to the engine, which rasterises them on the GPU.

> TIP: You'll rarely touch elements or render objects directly, but knowing they exist explains Flutter's performance rules: keep `build` cheap, and use `const` widgets.

:::check Which tree holds State objects for StatefulWidgets?
The element tree.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Believing that rebuilding a widget recreates everything on screen. Elements and render objects are reused when possible.
- Doing expensive work inside `build`, which may run every frame.

## Summary
- Flutter has a Dart framework, a C++ engine and platform embedders.
- Impeller renders without shader-compilation jank.
- Widgets (configuration), elements (lifecycle and state) and render objects (layout and paint) form three trees.

## Further learning
- docs.flutter.dev ▸ Architectural overview
- docs.flutter.dev ▸ Impeller rendering engine
@quiz
? mcq: Which layer of Flutter is written mostly in C++?
- The framework
+ The engine
- The Material library
- Your app
> The engine (rendering, Dart runtime, text) is C++.
? mcq: Which tree performs layout and painting?
- Widget tree
- Element tree
+ RenderObject tree
- Package tree
> Render objects compute sizes, paint and hit test.
? tf: Creating new widget objects on every build is expensive, so Flutter apps should avoid rebuilding.
= false
> Widgets are lightweight configurations; elements and render objects are reused.
? fill: Flutter's modern renderer that avoids shader compilation jank is called ___.
= Impeller
> Impeller precompiles shaders.
? order: Put the frame pipeline stages in order.
~ Build
~ Layout
~ Paint
~ Composite
> Widgets are built, laid out, painted, then composited and rasterised.
? scenario: A teammate puts a slow JSON parse inside build(). Why is that a problem?
+ build can run very often, even every frame during animations, so the slow work repeats and causes jank
- build only runs once per app launch
- build runs on a separate isolate automatically
- JSON cannot be parsed in Flutter
> Keep build methods fast and side-effect free.

@lesson flutter-m01-l03 | Flutter vs native development | 10
## Learning objectives
- Compare Flutter with native Android/iOS and other cross-platform tools
- Understand trade-offs for performance, UI fidelity and platform access
- Decide when Flutter is a good fit

## The options
- **Native**: Kotlin/Jetpack Compose for Android and Swift/SwiftUI for iOS. Two codebases, best direct access to new platform APIs.
- **Flutter**: one Dart codebase, own rendering engine, compiled to native code.
- **React Native**: JavaScript/TypeScript with React, rendering through native components.
- **Kotlin Multiplatform**: share business logic in Kotlin; UI can be native or Compose Multiplatform.

## Strengths of Flutter
- **Single codebase** for UI and logic across six platforms.
- **Consistent look**: pixel-identical rendering everywhere.
- **Hot reload** for very fast iteration.
- **Performance**: AOT-compiled Dart and GPU rendering; typically 60–120 fps.
- **Rich widgets**: Material 3 and Cupertino sets out of the box.

## Trade-offs
- **App size**: a minimal release APK is a few megabytes larger than a minimal native app because it bundles the engine.
- **Brand-new platform APIs** may need a plugin or a small amount of native code (via platform channels) until a package supports them.
- **Platform look and feel**: Flutter renders its own widgets, so matching every native detail (or OS updates to native controls) requires care.

## Accessing native code
When you need a platform feature without an existing package, Flutter lets you call Kotlin/Swift through **platform channels** (Module 14):

```dart
import 'package:flutter/services.dart';

const _battery = MethodChannel('samples.flutter.dev/battery');

Future<int?> batteryLevel() async {
  try {
    return await _battery.invokeMethod<int>('getBatteryLevel');
  } on PlatformException {
    return null;
  }
}
```

## When to choose what
- Flutter: multi-platform apps, custom branded UI, small teams, fast iteration.
- Native: apps tightly bound to the newest platform APIs (for example, deep OS integrations on launch day), or teams already invested in native expertise.
- Many companies mix them: a native app with Flutter screens added via "add-to-app".

> TIP: Choose based on product needs and team skills, not hype. Prototype the riskiest feature first in the technology you're evaluating.

:::check What mechanism lets Flutter call native Kotlin or Swift code?
Platform channels (MethodChannel, EventChannel, etc.), or packages built on them.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Assuming cross-platform means "slow". Flutter compiles to native code.
- Assuming Flutter can never use native features; plugins and platform channels cover them.

## Summary
- Flutter offers one codebase, consistent rendering and fast iteration.
- Trade-offs include slightly larger binaries and occasional native code for new APIs.
- Platform channels and plugins bridge to native features.

## Further learning
- docs.flutter.dev ▸ Platform integration
- docs.flutter.dev ▸ Add Flutter to an existing app
@quiz
? mcq: How does Flutter render its UI?
+ With its own engine, drawing every pixel
- By converting widgets into native views
- Through a web view
- Using XML layouts
> Flutter's engine renders the UI directly.
? tf: Flutter apps can call native Kotlin or Swift code when needed.
= true
> Platform channels and plugins provide native access.
? mcq: Which is a realistic trade-off of Flutter?
+ A slightly larger minimal app size because the engine is bundled
- No support for iOS
- No hot reload
- Interpreted Dart in release builds
> The bundled engine adds a few megabytes to minimal apps.
? fill: Adding Flutter screens to an existing native app is called add-to-___.
= app
> Add-to-app integrates Flutter modules into native apps.
? scenario: A company needs an app on Android, iOS and desktop with a highly custom design and only three developers. What's a strong option?
+ Flutter, sharing one codebase with custom rendering
- Three separate native apps
- A command-line tool
- A static website only
> Flutter's single codebase and custom UI suit small multi-platform teams.

@lesson flutter-m01-l04 | Installing the Flutter SDK | 14
## Learning objectives
- Install the Flutter SDK on Windows, macOS or Linux
- Add Flutter to your PATH and verify the installation
- Understand Flutter channels and upgrades

## System requirements (overview)
- 64-bit Windows 10+, macOS, or a 64-bit Linux distribution
- Several GB of free disk space (SDK, Android SDK, emulators)
- Git installed
- For iOS development: a Mac with Xcode

## Install
The recommended path changes over time, so always follow docs.flutter.dev for your OS. The two common approaches are:

1. **Using VS Code**: install VS Code and the Flutter extension; it can download the SDK for you ("Flutter: New Project" offers to install it).
2. **Manual download**: download the stable SDK archive for your OS, extract it to a folder without spaces (for example `C:\dev\flutter` or `~/development/flutter`), and add `flutter/bin` to your PATH.

```bash
# macOS / Linux (zsh or bash) – add to ~/.zshrc or ~/.bashrc
export PATH="$HOME/development/flutter/bin:$PATH"

# Then open a new terminal
flutter --version
```

On Windows, add `C:\dev\flutter\bin` to the **Path** user environment variable through System Properties ▸ Environment Variables, then open a new terminal.

## Verify
```bash
flutter --version
# Flutter 3.x.y • channel stable • https://github.com/flutter/flutter.git
# Dart 3.x.y • DevTools 2.x.y
```

The output shows the Flutter version **and** the bundled Dart version.

## Channels and upgrades
```bash
flutter channel          # show channels; use 'stable'
flutter upgrade          # upgrade to the latest version of your channel
flutter downgrade        # return to the previous version
```

- **stable**: recommended for production and learning.
- **beta**: monthly previews of upcoming features.
- **master/main**: the very latest commits, can break.

## Disable analytics (optional)
```bash
flutter --disable-analytics
```

> WARNING: Don't install Flutter in a folder that requires administrator rights (like `C:\Program Files`) or contains spaces; it causes permission and path issues.

:::check Which folder must be on your PATH for the flutter command to work?
The bin folder inside the Flutter SDK directory (flutter/bin).
:::

## Common mistakes
- Forgetting to restart the terminal or IDE after editing PATH.
- Installing into a protected folder or a path with spaces.

## Exercise
Install Flutter, then run `flutter --version`. Note the Flutter and Dart versions.
:::solution What success looks like
```bash
$ flutter --version
Flutter 3.29.2 • channel stable
Dart 3.7.2 • DevTools 2.42.3
```
Any recent stable version is fine for this course.
:::

## Summary
- Install the stable SDK via VS Code or by extracting the archive.
- Add `flutter/bin` to PATH and verify with `flutter --version`.
- Stay on the stable channel; update with `flutter upgrade`.

## Further learning
- docs.flutter.dev ▸ Get started ▸ Install
@quiz
? mcq: Which channel should you use for production apps?
+ stable
- beta
- master
- dev
> The stable channel receives tested releases.
? fill: To update Flutter to the newest release of your channel, run `flutter ___`.
= upgrade
> flutter upgrade updates the SDK.
? tf: `flutter --version` also shows the version of the bundled Dart SDK.
= true
> The output lists both Flutter and Dart versions.
? mcq: Which install location is a bad idea on Windows?
+ C:\Program Files\flutter
- C:\dev\flutter
- C:\src\flutter
- D:\tools\flutter
> Protected folders and paths with spaces cause problems.
? scenario: You added Flutter to PATH but `flutter` is still not recognised in VS Code's terminal. What should you try first?
+ Restart VS Code so it picks up the new PATH
- Reinstall Windows
- Switch to the master channel
- Delete pubspec.yaml
> Running programs keep their old environment until restarted.

@lesson flutter-m01-l05 | Android Studio setup | 12
## Learning objectives
- Install Android Studio and the Android SDK components Flutter needs
- Install the Flutter and Dart plugins
- Accept Android licenses

## Why Android Studio?
Even if you write code in VS Code, Flutter needs the **Android SDK**, platform tools and build tools to compile Android apps, and Android Studio is the easiest way to install them. It also includes the Android **Emulator**.

## Steps
1. Download and install **Android Studio** from developer.android.com.
2. Run the setup wizard and install the **Android SDK**, **Android SDK Platform-Tools** and **Android SDK Build-Tools**.
3. Open **SDK Manager ▸ SDK Tools** and also install **Android SDK Command-line Tools (latest)**.
4. Install the **Flutter** plugin (which also installs the **Dart** plugin) from **Settings ▸ Plugins ▸ Marketplace**, then restart.
5. Accept the Android licences from a terminal:

```bash
flutter doctor --android-licenses
# Press y to accept each licence
```

## Creating a project in Android Studio
**File ▸ New ▸ New Flutter Project**, choose Flutter, check that the SDK path points to your Flutter folder, then pick a project name (lowercase_with_underscores), organisation (reverse domain like `com.example`) and platforms.

## Useful features
- **Flutter Inspector**: visualise and explore the widget tree, highlight repaints.
- **Hot reload** button (⚡) and **Hot restart**.
- **Run/Debug** configurations with breakpoints.
- **Refactor ▸ Wrap with widget** (Alt+Enter / ⌥⏎ on a widget) to wrap it in Padding, Center, Column and more.

```dart
// Place the cursor on Text, press Alt+Enter (⌥⏎ on macOS) and choose
// "Wrap with Padding" to produce:
// Padding(
//   padding: const EdgeInsets.all(8.0),
//   child: Text('Hello'),
// )
```

## Point Flutter to the SDK (if needed)
If `flutter doctor` can't find the Android SDK:

```bash
flutter config --android-sdk /path/to/Android/sdk
```

> TIP: Keep Android Studio updated, but don't upgrade it in the middle of a release crunch; new versions occasionally require Gradle or plugin updates.

:::check Which command accepts the Android SDK licences?
flutter doctor --android-licenses
:::

## Common mistakes
- Skipping the Command-line Tools component, which `flutter doctor` reports as missing.
- Installing the Flutter plugin but not restarting Android Studio.

## Summary
- Android Studio installs the Android SDK, tools and emulator Flutter needs.
- Install the Flutter and Dart plugins and accept licences.
- Use the Flutter Inspector, hot reload and "wrap with widget" refactorings.

## Further learning
- docs.flutter.dev ▸ Get started ▸ Install ▸ Android setup
@quiz
? fill: Accept Android licences with `flutter doctor --android-___`.
= licenses | licences
> The command walks you through each licence.
? mcq: Which SDK component does flutter doctor often report missing if you skip it?
+ Android SDK Command-line Tools
- Kotlin compiler
- Gradle wrapper
- Java EE
> The command-line tools are needed for licence management and builds.
? tf: Installing the Flutter plugin in Android Studio also installs the Dart plugin.
= true
> The Flutter plugin depends on and installs the Dart plugin.
? mcq: Which Android Studio tool lets you explore the live widget tree?
+ Flutter Inspector
- Layout Editor
- Logcat
- APK Analyzer
> The Flutter Inspector visualises widgets.
? scenario: flutter doctor says it can't locate the Android SDK, which you installed in a custom folder. What helps?
+ flutter config --android-sdk <path>
- Reinstall Flutter
- Delete the emulator
- Switch to the beta channel
> flutter config can point to a custom SDK location.

@lesson flutter-m01-l06 | VS Code setup | 10
## Learning objectives
- Configure VS Code for Flutter development
- Use the command palette, device selector and debugging tools
- Turn on productivity settings

## Install the extension
1. Install **Visual Studio Code**.
2. Open the Extensions view and install **Flutter** (by Dart Code). It installs the **Dart** extension too.
3. Open the Command Palette (**Ctrl+Shift+P** / **⌘⇧P**) and run **Flutter: Run Flutter Doctor** to confirm everything works.

## Create and run a project
- Command Palette ▸ **Flutter: New Project** ▸ **Application**.
- Pick a device in the status bar (bottom right): an emulator, a connected phone, Chrome or your desktop.
- Press **F5** to run with debugging, or **Ctrl+F5** to run without.

## Recommended settings
Add these to your user `settings.json`:

```json
{
  "editor.formatOnSave": true,
  "editor.codeActionsOnSave": {
    "source.fixAll": "explicit"
  },
  "dart.previewFlutterUiGuides": true,
  "[dart]": {
    "editor.rulers": [80],
    "editor.tabSize": 2
  }
}
```

- **Format on save** runs `dart format`.
- **fixAll** applies analyzer quick fixes (like adding `const`) on save.
- **UI guides** draw lines connecting parent and child widgets in the editor.

## Handy shortcuts
- **Ctrl+.** / **⌘.**: quick fixes and refactors such as *Wrap with widget*, *Extract widget*, *Remove widget*.
- **F12**: go to definition (great for reading Flutter's source).
- Save the file to trigger **hot reload** while debugging.

## Snippets
Type `stless` or `stful` and press Enter to generate a stateless or stateful widget skeleton:

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class ProfileCard extends StatelessWidget {
  const ProfileCard({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return const Placeholder();
  }
}
```

> TIP: Use **Flutter: Open DevTools** from the Command Palette to inspect widgets, performance and memory while your app runs.

:::check Which shortcut opens quick fixes such as "Wrap with Center"?
Ctrl+. on Windows/Linux or ⌘. on macOS.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Installing only the Dart extension and missing Flutter-specific commands.
- Running the app from a terminal and wondering why breakpoints in VS Code don't stop; launch it with F5 instead.

## Summary
- Install the Flutter extension and run Flutter Doctor from the Command Palette.
- Choose devices in the status bar and run with F5.
- Enable format on save, fix-all on save and UI guides; use Ctrl+. refactors and widget snippets.

## Further learning
- docs.flutter.dev ▸ Tools ▸ VS Code
@quiz
? mcq: What does the snippet `stless` generate?
+ A StatelessWidget class skeleton
- A StatefulWidget with State
- A test file
- A pubspec.yaml
> stless creates a stateless widget template; stful creates a stateful one.
? tf: Saving a file while debugging in VS Code triggers hot reload.
= true
> The Flutter extension hot reloads on save during debug sessions.
? fill: To run a Flutter app with debugging in VS Code, press ___.
= F5
> F5 starts debugging.
? mcq: Which setting automatically formats Dart code when you save?
+ "editor.formatOnSave": true
- "dart.lint": true
- "flutter.format": "auto"
- "editor.wordWrap": "on"
> formatOnSave runs the Dart formatter.
? scenario: You want to quickly surround a Text widget with Padding without typing brackets. What do you use?
+ The Ctrl+. (⌘.) quick fix "Wrap with Padding"
- Manually retype the whole tree
- Hot restart
- flutter clean
> The wrap refactor inserts the parent widget for you.

@lesson flutter-m01-l07 | Flutter Doctor | 8
## Learning objectives
- Run `flutter doctor` and read its report
- Fix common issues it reports
- Use verbose mode for troubleshooting

## Your setup's health check
`flutter doctor` checks your environment and reports what's ready and what's missing:

```bash
flutter doctor
```

```text
Doctor summary (to see all details, run flutter doctor -v):
[✓] Flutter (Channel stable, 3.x.y)
[✓] Android toolchain - develop for Android devices (Android SDK version 35.0.0)
[✗] Xcode - develop for iOS and macOS
    ✗ Xcode installation is incomplete
[✓] Chrome - develop for the web
[✓] Android Studio (version 2024.x)
[✓] VS Code (version 1.9x)
[✓] Connected device (2 available)
[✓] Network resources
```

- **✓** ready
- **!** works, with warnings
- **✗** missing or broken

You don't need every line green. If you only build Android apps, a missing Xcode or Visual Studio entry is fine.

## Common issues and fixes
- **Android licences not accepted** → `flutter doctor --android-licenses`
- **cmdline-tools component is missing** → install *Android SDK Command-line Tools* in Android Studio's SDK Manager
- **Unable to locate Android SDK** → `flutter config --android-sdk <path>`
- **No devices available** → start an emulator or connect a phone with USB debugging
- **Network resources unavailable** → check proxy/firewall settings

## Verbose mode
```bash
flutter doctor -v
```

Prints paths, versions and detailed diagnostics. Paste this output when asking for help online.

## Useful companions
```bash
flutter devices          # list connected devices and emulators
flutter emulators        # list available emulators
flutter clean            # delete build outputs when builds behave strangely
```

> TIP: Run `flutter doctor` after every major tool upgrade (Flutter, Android Studio, Xcode). It catches broken setups before they waste your afternoon.

:::check Do you need Xcode for Android-only development?
No. Missing Xcode only affects iOS/macOS builds.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Trying to fix every ✗ even for platforms you don't target.
- Asking for help without sharing `flutter doctor -v` output.

## Summary
- `flutter doctor` reports the health of your Flutter setup.
- Fix issues for the platforms you target; others can stay unchecked.
- Use `-v` for details; `flutter devices` and `flutter emulators` help with devices.

## Further learning
- docs.flutter.dev ▸ Get started ▸ Troubleshooting
@quiz
? mcq: Which command shows detailed diagnostics of your Flutter setup?
+ flutter doctor -v
- flutter analyze
- flutter run --debug
- flutter pub deps
> -v enables verbose output.
? tf: Every item in flutter doctor must show a check mark before you can build Android apps.
= false
> Only the toolchain for your target platforms needs to be ready.
? fill: To list connected devices and emulators, run `flutter ___`.
= devices
> flutter devices lists available targets.
? scenario: flutter doctor reports "Android license status unknown". What do you run?
+ flutter doctor --android-licenses
- flutter clean
- flutter upgrade --force
- flutter config --no-analytics
> Accepting the licences fixes this report.
? mcq: What does the ! symbol mean in flutter doctor output?
+ The component works but has warnings
- The component is fully ready
- The component is not supported
- The command crashed
> ! indicates a partially working component.

@lesson flutter-m01-l08 | Emulators and devices | 12
## Learning objectives
- Create and launch an Android emulator
- Run apps on a physical Android device
- Choose the right device for each task

## Android Virtual Devices (AVDs)
1. In Android Studio, open **Device Manager**.
2. Click **Create Virtual Device**, pick a phone (for example *Pixel 8*), then a **system image** (choose a recent API level with Google Play if you need Play services).
3. Finish and press ▶ to boot it.

From the terminal:

```bash
flutter emulators                      # list emulators
flutter emulators --launch Pixel_8_API_35
flutter run                            # runs on the running emulator
```

> TIP: Enable **hardware acceleration** (Intel HAXM/WHPX on Windows, Hypervisor Framework on macOS, KVM on Linux). Without it, emulators are painfully slow.

## Physical Android devices
1. On the phone: **Settings ▸ About phone**, tap **Build number** seven times to enable Developer options.
2. In **Developer options**, enable **USB debugging**.
3. Connect via USB and accept the "Allow USB debugging?" prompt.
4. Run `flutter devices` to confirm it's listed.

Wireless debugging (Android 11+) works too: **Developer options ▸ Wireless debugging**, then pair the device from Android Studio.

## iOS
On a Mac with Xcode, `open -a Simulator` starts the iOS Simulator. Running on a physical iPhone requires an Apple developer account and code signing setup in Xcode.

## Choosing a device
- **Emulator/simulator**: quick to start, easy to test different screen sizes and Android versions.
- **Physical device**: real performance, camera, sensors, notifications and touch feel. Always test release builds on real hardware before publishing.
- **Chrome / desktop**: fast iteration for layout work, but behaviour differs from mobile.

## Selecting a target
```bash
flutter run -d emulator-5554   # by device id
flutter run -d chrome          # web
flutter run -d windows         # desktop (if enabled)
```

> WARNING: Performance in debug mode is much slower than release. Measure performance with `flutter run --profile` on a real device.

:::check Where do you enable USB debugging on Android?
In Developer options, which you unlock by tapping Build number seven times.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Judging app performance from debug builds on an emulator.
- Forgetting to accept the USB debugging prompt on the phone.

## Summary
- Create AVDs in Android Studio's Device Manager; launch them from the IDE or `flutter emulators`.
- Enable Developer options and USB debugging for physical devices.
- Test real performance in profile/release mode on real hardware.

## Further learning
- developer.android.com ▸ Run apps on the Android Emulator
- docs.flutter.dev ▸ Testing ▸ Debugging
@quiz
