+ A physical device running a profile or release build
- The web build in Chrome
- An emulator in debug mode
- A desktop build
> Real hardware is needed for sensors, camera and true performance.

@lesson flutter-m01-l09 | Your first Flutter app | 16
## Learning objectives
- Create and run a new Flutter project
- Understand the counter app's code line by line
- Use hot reload and hot restart

## Create and run
```bash
flutter create first_app
cd first_app
flutter run
```

The template is the famous **counter app**: a button that increments a number on screen.

## The code, simplified
```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

void main() {
  runApp(const MyApp());
}

class MyApp extends StatelessWidget {
  const MyApp({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return MaterialApp(
      title: 'First App',
      theme: ThemeData(colorSchemeSeed: Colors.deepPurple),
      home: const CounterPage(title: 'Counter'),
    );
  }
}

class CounterPage extends StatefulWidget {
  const CounterPage({super.key, required this.title});
  final String title;

  @override
  State<CounterPage> createState() => _CounterPageState();
}

class _CounterPageState extends State<CounterPage> {
  int _counter = 0;

  void _increment() {
    setState(() {
      _counter++;
    });
  }

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(title: Text(widget.title)),
      body: Center(
        child: Column(
          mainAxisAlignment: MainAxisAlignment.center,
          children: [
            const Text('You have pushed the button this many times:'),
            Text('$_counter', style: Theme.of(context).textTheme.headlineMedium),
          ],
        ),
      ),
      floatingActionButton: FloatingActionButton(
        onPressed: _increment,
        tooltip: 'Increment',
        child: const Icon(Icons.add),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

## Line by line
- `runApp(const MyApp())` inflates the root widget and attaches it to the screen.
- `MyApp` is a **StatelessWidget**: it has no changing data, just configuration (title, theme, home).
- `MaterialApp` sets up Material Design, theming and navigation.
- `CounterPage` is a **StatefulWidget**: it creates a `State` object that holds `_counter`.
- `setState` tells Flutter "my state changed, please call `build` again". Flutter then updates the number on screen.
- `widget.title` accesses the StatefulWidget's fields from its State.
- `Scaffold` provides the app bar, body and floating action button slots.
- `Theme.of(context)` reads the current theme from the widget tree.

## Hot reload vs hot restart
- **Hot reload** (`r` in the terminal, or save in the IDE): injects updated code and rebuilds, **keeping state**. Change the text or a colour and the counter value stays.
- **Hot restart** (`R`): restarts the app from `main`, **resetting state**. Needed when you change `main`, global variables, or the type of state fields.
- **Full restart** (stop and run again): required after adding native plugins or changing native code.

## Try it
1. Change `Colors.deepPurple` to `Colors.teal` and hot reload: the theme changes, the count stays.
2. Change `_counter++` to `_counter += 2`, hot reload, and press the button.

> TIP: If hot reload doesn't show your change, ask: did I change something that only runs once (like `main` or `initState`)? Then hot restart.

:::check What happens if you change _counter++ without calling setState?
The variable changes, but Flutter doesn't rebuild, so the screen keeps showing the old number.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Updating state without `setState`.
- Expecting hot reload to re-run `main()` or `initState()`.

## Summary
- `flutter create` + `flutter run` start a project.
- StatelessWidgets describe fixed UI; StatefulWidgets keep mutable state in a State object.
- `setState` triggers a rebuild; hot reload keeps state, hot restart resets it.

## Further learning
- docs.flutter.dev ▸ Get started ▸ Write your first app (codelab)
@quiz
? mcq: Which function attaches the root widget to the screen?
+ runApp
- startApp
- setState
- build
> runApp inflates the root widget.
? tf: Hot reload resets all state in the app.
= false
> Hot reload keeps state; hot restart resets it.
? fill: To tell Flutter that state changed and the UI must rebuild, call ___.
= setState
> setState schedules a rebuild of the State's widget.
? mcq: How does a State object read fields declared on its StatefulWidget?
+ Through widget.fieldName
- Through this.parent
- Through context.fields
- It cannot
> State has a widget property referencing its current widget.
? scenario: You changed code inside main() but hot reload shows no difference. What should you do?
+ Hot restart, because main only runs at startup
- Run flutter clean
- Reinstall the SDK
- Restart the computer
> Hot reload doesn't re-run main; hot restart does.
? order: Arrange the commands to create and run a new app.
~ flutter create first_app
~ cd first_app
~ flutter run
> Create the project, enter its folder, then run it.

@lesson flutter-m01-l10 | Project folder structure | 12
## Learning objectives
- Navigate a Flutter project's folders and files
- Know which files you edit and which are generated
- Organise lib/ for growing apps

## What flutter create generates
```diagram
my_app/
├── android/          Android host project (Gradle, Kotlin, manifest)
├── ios/              iOS host project (Xcode, Info.plist)
├── web/              index.html, icons, manifest.json
├── windows/ macos/ linux/   desktop hosts (if enabled)
├── lib/
│   └── main.dart     ← your Dart code starts here
├── test/
│   └── widget_test.dart
├── pubspec.yaml      ← name, version, dependencies, assets, fonts
├── pubspec.lock      ← exact resolved versions (commit for apps)
├── analysis_options.yaml ← lint rules (flutter_lints)
├── .dart_tool/       generated, don't edit or commit
└── build/            build outputs, don't commit
```

## Files you'll edit often
- **lib/**: all your Dart code.
- **pubspec.yaml**: dependencies, assets and fonts.
- **test/**: your tests.
- **android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml**: permissions, app name, deep links.
- **ios/Runner/Info.plist**: iOS permissions descriptions and settings.

## Declaring assets
Images, JSON files and fonts must be declared in `pubspec.yaml`:

```yaml
flutter:
  uses-material-design: true
  assets:
    - assets/images/
    - assets/data/lessons.json
  fonts:
    - family: Inter
      fonts:
        - asset: assets/fonts/Inter-Regular.ttf
        - asset: assets/fonts/Inter-Bold.ttf
          weight: 700
```

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class Logo extends StatelessWidget {
  const Logo({super.key});
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) => Image.asset('assets/images/logo.png', width: 96);
}
```

> WARNING: YAML is indentation-sensitive. Use two spaces, never tabs, and keep `assets:` nested under `flutter:`.

## Organising lib/ as the app grows
Start simple, then group by **feature**:

```diagram
lib/
├── main.dart
├── app.dart                 MaterialApp, theme, routes
├── core/                    shared utilities, theme, constants
├── features/
│   ├── auth/
│   │   ├── data/            repositories, API clients
│   │   ├── domain/          models, use cases
│   │   └── presentation/    screens, widgets, state
│   └── lessons/ ...
└── shared/widgets/          reusable widgets
```

Feature-first organisation keeps related code together; Module 15 covers architecture in depth.

## What to commit
Commit everything except generated folders: `build/`, `.dart_tool/`, IDE folders and secrets. The generated `.gitignore` already handles the common cases.

:::check Where do you declare an image so Image.asset can load it?
Under the assets section of flutter: in pubspec.yaml.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Forgetting to declare assets, causing "Unable to load asset" errors.
- Committing `build/` or API keys.

## Summary
- Platform folders host your app on each OS; your Dart code lives in `lib/`.
- `pubspec.yaml` declares dependencies, assets and fonts.
- Organise `lib/` by feature as the app grows; don't commit generated folders.

## Further learning
- docs.flutter.dev ▸ UI ▸ Assets and images
@quiz
? mcq: Where does your Dart application code live?
+ lib/
- android/
- build/
- .dart_tool/
> lib/ contains your Dart source, starting with main.dart.
? tf: The build/ folder should be committed to version control.
= false
> build/ contains generated outputs.
? fill: Images and fonts are declared in the ___ file.
= pubspec.yaml
> The flutter section of pubspec.yaml lists assets and fonts.
? mcq: Which file would you edit to add an Android permission?
+ android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml
- lib/main.dart
- pubspec.lock
- web/index.html
> Android permissions are declared in the manifest.
? scenario: Image.asset throws "Unable to load asset" for a file that exists on disk. What's the most likely cause?
+ The asset isn't declared (or is mis-indented) in pubspec.yaml
- The image is too small
- Hot reload is disabled
- The device is offline
> Undeclared assets are not bundled into the app.
